window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-physics-work-energy-power-work-energy-theorem-conservative-forces-power"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
<h1>Mechanics – Work, Energy & Power</h1>

<h2>1. Foundations: Physical Quantities & Units</h2>
<p><strong>Physical Quantities</strong></p>
<p>All the quantities which can be measured directly or indirectly in terms of which law of physics are described and whose measurement is necessary, are called <span style='color: var(--warning);'>physical quantities</span>.</p>
<p>e.g., velocity, time, mass, force, energy, power, etc.</p>

<p><strong>Unit</strong></p>
<p>The standard amount of a physical quantity chosen to measure the physical quantity of same kind is called a <span style='color: var(--warning);'>physical unit</span>. The International System of Units (SI) is the universally accepted system for the Indian defence examinations.</p>

<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>SI Unit</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Length</td><td>metre</td><td>m</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mass</td><td>kilogram</td><td>kg</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Time</td><td>second</td><td>s</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Force</td><td>newton</td><td>N</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Energy</td><td>joule</td><td>J</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Power</td><td>watt</td><td>W</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2. Work – The First Step Toward Energy</h2>
<h3>2.1 Definition & Concept</h3>
<p>Work is done when a force causes a displacement of an object in the direction of the force. It is a scalar quantity.</p>
<p><strong>Mathematical expression (constant force):</strong></p>
<p><strong>W = F·d·cosθ</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>F</strong> – magnitude of the constant force (N)</li>
  <li><strong>d</strong> – displacement of the point of application (m)</li>
  <li>θ – angle between force and displacement vectors</li>
</ul>

<p>For variable forces, work is obtained by integration:</p>
<p><strong>W = ∫ F·dr = ∫ F cosθ dr</strong></p>

<h3>2.2 Sign Convention</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Positive work</strong> – force component along displacement (θ < 90°).</li>
  <li><strong>Negative work</strong> – force component opposite to displacement (θ > 90°).</li>
  <li><strong>Zero work</strong> – force perpendicular to displacement (θ = 90°) or no displacement.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Work Done by Common Forces</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Force Type</th><th>Work Expression</th><th>Typical Example</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Constant horizontal force</td><td>W = F d</td><td>Pushing a crate on a smooth floor</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gravity (near Earth)</td><td>W = m g h (if upward)</td><td>Lifting a weight</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Spring (Hooke’s law)</td><td>W = ½ k x² (when stretched/compressed from natural length)</td><td>Compressing a spring</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Friction</td><td>W = -f d</td><td>Sliding a block on a rough surface</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2.4 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>Always check the direction of the force relative to displacement. A common mistake in NDA/CDS is to forget the cosine factor, especially when the force is at an angle. Write “W = F d cosθ” on the first line of your answer; it saves marks.</p>

<h2>3. Kinetic Energy – Energy of Motion</h2>
<h3>3.1 Definition</h3>
<p>Kinetic energy (KE) is the energy possessed by a body due to its motion.</p>

<p><strong>Kinetic energy:</strong> Particles of matter are moving continuously.</p>
<p><strong>Temperature relationship:</strong> Temperature <span style='color: var(--warning);'>↑</span> - Kinetic Energy (K.E) of particle <span style='color: var(--warning);'>↑</span></p>
<p><strong>Kinetic energy:</strong> Particles of matter are moving continuously.</p>
<p><strong>Temperature:</strong> As temperature increases, the Kinetic Energy (K.E) of particles increases.</p>

<h3>3.2 Derivation from Work–Energy Theorem</h3>
<p>Consider a particle of mass <strong>m</strong> acted upon by a constant net force <strong>F</strong> along the direction of motion. Using Newton’s second law, <strong>F = m a</strong>. The work done over a displacement <strong>s</strong> is:</p>
<p>W = F s = m a s.</p>
<p>From kinematics, <strong>a s = (v² - u²)/2</strong> where <strong>u</strong> and <strong>v</strong> are initial and final speeds respectively. Substituting:</p>
<p>W = m (v² - u²)/2 = ½ m v² - ½ m u².</p>
<p>Hence, the change in kinetic energy ΔKE = KE<sub>final</sub> – KE<sub>initial</sub> equals the net work done:</p>
<p><strong>ΔKE = W</strong></p>

<h3>3.3 Formula</h3>
<p><strong>KE = ½ m v²</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>m – mass (kg)</li>
  <li>v – speed (m s⁻¹)</li>
  <li>½ is a constant; highlight as <span style='color: var(--warning);'>½</span> for exam clarity.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.4 Sample Calculations</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Mass (kg)</th><th>Speed (m s⁻¹)</th><th>Kinetic Energy (J)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>½·1·10² = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>50</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>15</td><td>½·2·15² = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>225</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>0.5</td><td>20</td><td>½·0.5·20² = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.5 Mnemonic</h3>
<p>“<strong>KE = ½ m v²</strong> – **K**inetic **E**nergy is **Half** the **Mass** times **Velocity Squared**.”</p>

<h3>3.6 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>When a question asks for “the work done in bringing a moving body to rest,” directly use ΔKE = -½ m v². No need to compute intermediate forces unless explicitly asked.</p>

<h2>4. Potential Energy & Conservative Forces</h2>
<h3>4.1 Conservative Forces – Definition</h3>
<p>A force is called <strong>conservative</strong> if the work done by it on a particle moving between two points is independent of the path taken. Equivalently, the work done over any closed loop is zero.</p>

<h3>4.2 Common Conservative Forces</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Gravitational force</strong> (near Earth: F = m g, direction downward)</li>
  <li><strong>Elastic (spring) force</strong> (Hooke’s law: F = –k x)</li>
  <li><strong>Electrostatic force</strong> (Coulomb’s law)</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3 Potential Energy (PE) – General Definition</h3>
<p>Potential energy associated with a conservative force **F** is defined as the negative of the work done by the force when the particle moves from a reference point **A** to a point **B**:</p>
<p><strong>U(B) – U(A) = –∫<sub>A</sub><sup>B</sup> F·dr</strong></p>
<p>Choosing the reference point where U = 0 simplifies calculations.</p>

<h3>4.4 Specific Forms of Potential Energy</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Force</th><th>Potential Energy Expression</th><th>Reference Point</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Gravity (near Earth)</td><td>U = m g h</td><td>h = 0 at ground level</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gravity (general)</td><td>U = –G M m / r</td><td>U = 0 at r → ∞</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Spring</td><td>U = ½ k x²</td><td>x = 0 at natural length</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>4.5 Conservative vs Non‑Conservative Forces – Quick Comparison</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Conservative</th><th>Non‑Conservative</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Work depends on path?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Closed‑loop work</td><td>Zero</td><td>Non‑zero (e.g., friction)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Associated scalar function</td><td>Potential Energy (U)</td><td>None</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Energy conversion</td><td>Mechanical energy conserved</td><td>Mechanical energy dissipated</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>4.6 Mnemonic for Conservative Forces</h3>
<p>“**G**ravity, **E**lastic, **C**oulomb – **GEC** are **C**onservative.”</p>

<h3>4.7 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>Whenever a problem mentions “no friction” or “only gravity acts,” immediately invoke mechanical energy conservation: <strong>KE<sub>i</sub> + PE<sub>i</sub> = KE<sub>f</sub> + PE<sub>f</sub></strong>. This saves time on force analysis.</p>

<h2>5. Mechanical Energy – The Sum of KE and PE</h2>
<h3>5.1 Definition</h3>
<p>Mechanical energy (E) of a system is the algebraic sum of its kinetic and potential energies:</p>
<p><strong>E = KE + PE</strong></p>

<h3>5.2 Conservation Principle</h3>
<p>If only conservative forces do work, the total mechanical energy remains constant:</p>
<p><strong>E<sub>initial</sub> = E<sub>final</sub></strong></p>

<h3>5.3 Example: Projectile Motion (Neglecting Air Resistance)</h3>
<p>At launch (height = 0):</p>
<p>KE<sub>i</sub> = ½ m v₀², PE<sub>i</sub> = 0</p>
<p>At the highest point (vertical speed = 0):</p>
<p>KE<sub>f</sub> = ½ m v<sub>horizontal</sub>², PE<sub>f</sub> = m g h<sub>max</sub></p>
<p>Equating total energy gives the classic range and height formulas.</p>

<h3>5.4 Energy Lost to Non‑Conservative Forces</h3>
<p>If friction or air resistance is present, the work done by these forces (W<sub>nc</sub>) appears as a loss:</p>
<p><strong>E<sub>final</sub> = E<sub>initial</sub> + W<sub>nc</sub></strong> (where W<sub>nc</sub> is negative).</p>

<h3>5.5 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>In multi‑step problems, write a “Energy Ledger” table: list each stage, KE, PE, and any work done by non‑conservative forces. This visual aid prevents omission of terms.</p>

<h2>6. Power – Rate of Doing Work</h2>
<h3>6.1 Definition</h3>
<p>Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.</p>
<p><strong>P = dW/dt</strong></p>

<h3>6.2 Forms of Power</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Average Power</strong>: <strong>P<sub>avg</sub> = ΔW / Δt = ΔE / Δt</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Instantaneous Power</strong>: <strong>P = F·v·cosθ</strong> (product of force component along velocity and speed)</li>
</ul>

<h3>6.3 Units</h3>
<p>SI unit of power is the watt (W), where 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹.</p>

<h3>6.4 Sample Calculations</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Given</th><th>Power (W)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Lifting a 10 kg box 5 m in 2 s</td><td>W = m g h = 10·9.8·5 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>490</span> J</td><td>P<sub>avg</sub> = 490/2 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>245</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Car engine delivering 60 kW</td><td>Given directly</td><td>60 kW = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>60 000</span> W</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Motor pulling a load with force 200 N at 3 m s⁻¹</td><td>P = F·v = 200·3 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>600</span> W</td><td>600 W</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>6.5 Mnemonic for Power Forms</h3>
<p>“**P** = **W**/t = **F**·**v** = Δ**E**/Δt.” Remember the three equalities.</p>

<h3>6.6 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>When a question provides force and distance but asks for power, first compute work (W = F·d·cosθ) and then divide by the given time. Do not forget to keep consistent units (Joules and seconds).</p>

<h2>7. Work‑Energy Theorem – The Bridge Between Work and Energy</h2>
<h3>7.1 Statement</h3>
<p>The net work done by all forces acting on a particle equals the change in its kinetic energy:</p>
<p><strong>W<sub>net</sub> = ΔKE = KE<sub>final</sub> – KE<sub>initial</sub></strong></p>

<h3>7.2 Derivation (From Newton’s Second Law)</h3>
<p>Start with **F = m a**. Multiply both sides by the infinitesimal displacement **dr**:</p>
<p>F·dr = m a·dr.</p>
<p>Since **a = dv/dt** and **dr = v dt**, we have:</p>
<p>F·dr = m v·dv.</p>
<p>Integrating from initial to final states:</p>
<p>∫F·dr = m∫v dv ⇒ W = ½ m v²|<sub>i</sub><sup>f</sup> = KE<sub>f</sub> – KE<sub>i</sub>.</p>

<h3>7.3 Applications</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Finding final speed of a body sliding down an inclined plane with friction.</li>
  <li>Determining the minimum force required to move a block over a rough surface.</li>
  <li>Analyzing particle motion in a variable force field (e.g., spring).</li>
</ul>

<h3>7.4 Example Problem (NDA Style)</h3>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A 2 kg block is pulled horizontally with a constant force of 15 N over 4 m. The coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.2. Find the final speed if the block starts from rest.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Calculate friction force: f = μ m g = 0.2·2·9.8 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3.92</span> N.</li>
  <li>Net force = 15 – 3.92 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>11.08</span> N.</li>
  <li>Work done by net force: W = F<sub>net</sub>·d = 11.08·4 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>44.32</span> J.</li>
  <li>Using work‑energy theorem: W = ΔKE = ½ m v² ⇒ 44.32 = ½·2·v² ⇒ v² = 44.32 ⇒ v = √44.32 ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>6.66</span> m s⁻¹.</li>
</ol>
<p>Answer: **≈ 6.7 m s⁻¹**.</p>

<h3>7.5 Exam Tip</h3>
<p>When both conservative and non‑conservative forces act, split the work term: <strong>W<sub>net</sub> = W<sub>cons</sub> + W<sub>nc</sub></strong>. Replace <strong>W<sub>cons</sub></strong> with –ΔPE and combine with KE to use the energy‑conservation form.</p>

<h2>8. Summary of Key Formulas</h2>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
  <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Formula</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Work (constant force)</td><td>W = F d cosθ</td><td>Scalar; sign depends on θ</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Work (variable force)</td><td>W = ∫ F·dr</td><td>Use appropriate limits</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Kinetic Energy</td><td>KE = ½ m v²</td><td>v = speed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gravitational PE (near Earth)</td><td>PE = m g h</td><td>h measured from reference</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Elastic PE</td><td>PE = ½ k x²</td><td>x = extension/compression</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mechanical Energy</td><td>E = KE + PE</td><td>Conserved if only conservative forces</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Power (average)</td><td>P<sub>avg</sub> = ΔW/Δt = ΔE/Δt</td><td>ΔW in joules, Δt in seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Power (instantaneous)</td><td>P = F·v·cosθ</td><td>Useful for rotating systems: P = τ·ω</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Work‑Energy Theorem</td><td>W<sub>net</sub> = ΔKE</td><td>Bridge between dynamics and energy</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Conservative Force Condition</td><td>∮ F·dr = 0</td><td>Closed‑loop work zero</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>9. Common Mistakes & How to Avoid Them (Exam‑Focused)</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Ignoring the cosine factor</strong> – Always write “cosθ” when force is not collinear with displacement.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixing up sign conventions</strong> – Positive work adds energy; negative work removes energy. Write a quick sign table before solving.</li>
  <li><strong>Using g = 10 m s⁻² indiscriminately</strong> – For NDA/CDS, g = 9.8 m s⁻² is preferred unless the question explicitly states otherwise.</li>
  <li><strong>Forgetting to convert units</strong> – Convert km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹ (multiply by 5/18) before plugging into KE formula.</li>
  <li><strong>Assuming frictionless when not mentioned</strong> – If the problem statement does not say “smooth” or “frictionless,” assume friction is present.</li>
  <li><strong>Overlooking reference level for PE</strong> – Clearly state where PE = 0; otherwise the answer may be off by a constant.</li>
  <li><strong>Using average speed instead of instantaneous speed for power</strong> – Power = F·v (instantaneous). If only average speed is given, compute average power, not instantaneous.</li>
</ol>

<h2>10. Quick Revision Mnemonics</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>WORK</strong>: <em>W = Force × Distance × cosθ</em> – “**W**e **F**orce **D**istance **C**osine.”</li>
  <li><strong>KE</strong>: “**K**inetic **E**nergy = **Half** **Mass** **Velocity‑Squared**.”</li>
  <li><strong>PE (Gravity)</strong>: “**P**otential **E**nergy = **Mass** **g** **Height**.”</li>
  <li><strong>POWER</strong>: “**P** = **W**/t = **F**·**v** = Δ**E**/Δt.”</li>
  <li><strong>CONSERVATIVE</strong>: “**GEC** – **G**ravity, **E**lastic, **C**oulomb – **C**onservative.”</li>
</ul>

<h2>11. Sample Integrated Problem (Full‑Syllabus)</h2>
<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A 0.5 kg ball is thrown vertically upward with an initial speed of 20 m s⁻¹ from ground level. Air resistance exerts a constant retarding force of 0.2 N opposite to the motion. Determine:</p>
<ol type="a">
  <li>Maximum height reached.</li>
  <li>Total time of flight.</li>
  <li>Average power delivered by the hand during the launch if the hand exerts the force over a distance of 0.5 m.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Solution Overview:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Use work‑energy theorem including non‑conservative work (air resistance).<br>
    <strong>W<sub>net</sub> = ΔKE = KE<sub>f</sub> – KE<sub>i</sub></strong>.<br>
    At the top, KE<sub>f</sub> = 0.<br>
    Net work = –½ m v₀² = –½·0.5·20² = –<span style='color: var(--warning);'>100</span> J.<br>
    Work done by gravity = –m g h (negative because gravity opposes upward motion).<br>
    Work done by air resistance = –F<sub>air</sub>·h = –0.2·h.<br>
    So, –100 = –m g h – 0.2 h ⇒ 100 = (0.5·9.8 + 0.2) h ⇒ 100 = (4.9 + 0.2) h = 5.1 h ⇒ h = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>19.6</span> m.</li>
  <li>Time to rise: use v = u – (g + F<sub>air</sub>/m) t.<br>
    Effective deceleration a = g + F<sub>air</sub>/m = 9.8 + 0.2/0.5 = 9.8 + 0.4 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10.2</span> m s⁻².<br>
    t<sub>up</sub> = u / a = 20 / 10.2 ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.96</span> s.<br>
    Total time = 2 t<sub>up</sub> ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3.92</span> s.</li>
  <li>Work done by hand = increase in mechanical energy (ignoring air resistance during the short push).<br>
    ΔE = KE<sub>i</sub> + PE at launch (PE = 0) = ½·0.5·20² = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100</span> J.<br>
    Distance over which hand acts = 0.5 m.<br>
    Average force by hand = ΔE / d = 100 / 0.5 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>200</span> N.<br>
    Average power = Force × average speed of hand. Approximate hand speed = d / t<sub>push</sub>. If push time ≈ 0.05 s (typical), speed ≈ 0.5 / 0.05 = 10 m s⁻¹.<br>
    P<sub>avg</sub> = 200 × 10 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2000</span> W = 2 kW.</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Key Take‑aways:</strong> Combine work‑energy theorem with non‑conservative work, treat air resistance as a constant retarding force, and remember to convert all quantities to SI before plugging into formulas.</p>

<h2>12. Final Checklist Before Submitting Answer Sheet</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Identify all forces (conservative vs non‑conservative).</li>
  <li>Write down the appropriate energy equation (KE + PE + W<sub>nc</sub> = constant).</li>
  <li>Check sign of each work term.</li>
  <li>Convert units (km h⁻¹ → m s⁻¹, g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless stated).</li>
  <li>State the reference level for potential energy.</li>
  <li>Include a brief statement of the principle used (e.g., “Using work‑energy theorem …”).</li>
  <li>Show final answer with correct number of significant figures (usually 2‑3 for NDA/CDS).</li>
</ul>

<h2>13. References (For Further Reading)</h2>
<ul>
  <li>HC Verma – Concepts of Physics Part‑1, Chapter 4.</li>
  <li>Resnick, Halliday & Walker – Fundamentals of Physics, Section on Work and Energy.</li>
  <li>Indian Defence Services Exam Guide – Energy & Power Chapter.</li>
</ul>

</div>
`;

